Anxiety in palliative care
Palliative & End of Life
Uncontrollable worry/dread, Panic attacks/autonomic arousal, Fear of the dying process

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-012
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of unremitting anxiety and restlessness causing severe somatic distress and preventing sleep.
Location: Residential Aged Care Facility
Present at Visit: Patient, registered nurse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage from the facility staff reported the patient was experiencing an acute episode of uncontrollable dread, profound restlessness, and severe diaphoresis. The patient's inability to settle and the severe autonomic arousal required urgent medical assessment to differentiate from acute physiological deterioration and to provide immediate pharmacological sedation to prevent complete exhaustion prior to the morning handover.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I can't stop shaking. Something terrible is about to happen, I know it, and the pain is going to be unbearable."
History of Present Illness: Over the last week, the patient has exhibited overwhelming, persistent, and completely uncontrollable worry regarding their disease progression. This evening, this culminated in frequent, severe panic attacks characterized by sudden, intense terror and profound autonomic arousal. The nursing staff report physical symptoms including severe tachycardia, profound diaphoresis, trembling, and noticeable shortness of breath. The patient complains of severe, unremitting tension localizing primarily to the muscles of the neck, back, and shoulders. There is profound generalized restlessness and an absolute, unremitting inability to physically or mentally relax. The patient verbalizes severe fear explicitly related to the dying process, specifically concerning uncontrolled pain or suffocation. Staff note distinct hypervigilance and a distinctly exaggerated, highly sensitive acoustic startle response to any noise in the hallway. There are severe sleep disturbances strictly characterized by profound difficulty falling asleep due to severe racing thoughts, which has exacerbated underlying physical symptoms precisely due to the uncontrollable emotional distress.
Functional Status: Bedbound but highly agitated, constantly shifting positions.
Social/Safety Concerns: Risk of self-injury from thrashing; severe disruption to other residents in the shared room.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 150/85 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 37.0 C | RR: 22 bpm | SpO2: 96% on room air | Wt: 55 Kg
General: Patient is agitated, hypervigilant, continuously wringing hands, and visibly trembling.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals a fine postural tremor in both hands and hyperreflexia without clonus. Cardiovascular exam shows tachycardia but no evidence of acute heart failure. Respiratory exam reveals rapid breathing but no adventitious sounds. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by the presentation of uncontrollable worry/dread (verbalized terror), panic attacks/autonomic arousal (tachycardia, tremor, sweating), and the explicit fear of the dying process.
Environmental Observations: The room environment is currently loud due to hallway activities, exacerbating the patient's acoustic startle response.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has missed doses of their baseline SSRI due to swallowing difficulties earlier in the week.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Anxiety in palliative care - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Hyperthyroidism (Thyroid storm) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of goiter, exophthalmos, and the acute, situational nature of the dread related to end-of-life thoughts.

Sepsis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fever, rigors, or localized signs of infection, and stable blood pressure.

Hypoxia secondary to pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to oxygen saturations of 96% on room air and clear bilateral air entry on auscultation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is suffering from an acute exacerbation of severe generalized anxiety and panic, directly related to existential end-of-life dread. The objective findings of tachycardia, tachypnea, and profound psychomotor restlessness confirm a severe state of autonomic hyperarousal. Because the patient is entirely unable to self-soothe and the resulting physical exhaustion is actively worsening their terminal trajectory, waiting for regular in-hours assessment would constitute a failure of basic palliative symptom control. Immediate after-hours medical intervention was required to safely prescribe and administer stat parenteral anxiolytics, as the patient's severe distress could not be safely managed by nursing staff under current PRN orders.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered stat subcutaneous clonazepam to achieve sustained anxiolysis and reduce the severe muscle tension. Adjusted baseline PRN orders to include scheduled low-dose antipsychotics for severe existential dread.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you hear loud noises in the hallway that trigger your startle reflex (trigger), press the call bell immediately so the nurse can close your door and play soft music (behavior). This will reduce the sensory overload and help prevent the panic cycle from starting (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Continued facility-based palliative care with a focus on a low-stimulation environment.
DME/Safety (Optional): Implementation of noise-cancelling headphones or a white noise machine at the bedside.
Follow-up: Facility GP review in the morning to formally adjust the baseline psychiatric medication regimen.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops worsening agitation that borders on severe delirium, attempts to climb out of bed, or if the current subcutaneous anxiolytics fail to induce calm within 60 minutes, the nursing staff must contact the after-hours medical service for further immediate sedation orders.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe anxiety is a common but highly treatable complication of the palliative journey; it is not a sign of personal weakness.
Clinical Course: Proactive, scheduled dosing of anti-anxiety medications will be strictly necessary moving forward to prevent these severe peaks of distress.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Nursing staff: Administer the PRN subcutaneous clonazepam at the earliest sign of restlessness or verbalized dread. Do not wait for full autonomic arousal to develop.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your nervous system is stuck in an overdrive state due to the completely understandable fear of your illness. The injection will bypass your stomach and directly calm the nerves, stopping the shaking and allowing you to finally rest.
Actionable Steps: Ask the staff to close the door to reduce hallway noise. Try to focus exclusively on the rhythm of your breathing when you feel the worry building.

SMS INFORMATION

